        <div id="discounts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What a Real Discount Looks Like in This Band</h2>
<p>
            Our method page puts genuine treadmill reductions in a strong sale window at roughly 15
            to 30% off the normal selling price, and says the mainstream mid-range is where the most
            useful of those reductions tend to appear. In this band that range has a specific
            meaning, and it is worth doing the arithmetic before the sale rather than during it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Start from the selling price, not the reference price.</strong> Horizon’s store
            showed the 7.0 AT against a $2,100 reference price in October while selling it for
            $1,099, or $934 with a code. NordicTrack listed the <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>T Series 10</a> at $1,599.99 against
            $1,799.99, while P.C. Richard sold the same model number for $1,299 on the same day. A
            Black Friday banner that measures its saving from $2,100 or $1,799.99 will show a large
            percentage on a price that may not have moved at all. Our <a href="/nordictrack-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Black Friday guide</a> follows the whole NordicTrack range through the sale.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Then apply the band.</strong> On a machine that normally sells for $1,099, a
            15% reduction is about $935 and a 30% reduction about $770. On a $1,299 machine, 15% is
            about $1,105 and 30% about $910. That is why the four running treadmills here are the
            interesting ones: each sits at a price where an ordinary, genuine Black Friday reduction
            takes it under $1,000, and none of them needs a doorbuster to get there. A price below
            the bottom of that band is possible, but it is the exception, and it is worth checking
            that the listing is for the current model before you trust it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use the dated prices you can find.</strong> Two of the machines here have
            recorded deal prices that make useful benchmarks. The Horizon 7.0 AT was posted at $899
            from Johnson Fitness and Wellness on Black Friday 2024 and at $800 from <a href="/dicks-sporting-goods-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Dick’s Sporting
            Goods</a> in June 2024. The NordicTrack T Series 10 was posted at $920 on Amazon on 27 June
            2026, against a $1,299 list. Neither is a promise about 2026, and both deals have
            expired, but they show where these machines have actually sold when retailers wanted to
            move them. A Black Friday price above those is not a bad price; it is just not an
            unusual one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Expect some of the saving to arrive as extras.</strong> Brands in this band often
            promote with included delivery, a membership period or accessories as well as, or
            instead of, a lower price. Count those at what you would have spent on them. A free
            month of iFIT is worth nothing if you were not going to subscribe, while upgraded
            delivery on a 250 to 330 lb box can be worth more than a further $50 off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Running Checks: 60-Inch Deck and 12 mph</h2>
<p>
            Under $1,000 you can buy a treadmill built for running, or a well-made walking machine
            that tolerates a jog, and the two often sit side by side in the same deal list at
            similar prices. Two figures separate them more reliably than anything else on the
            listing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck length: 60 inches for running.</strong> A running stride is longer than a
            walking one, and on a 55-inch deck most adults running at a steady pace use nearly all
            of it, which leaves no margin for a stride that drifts back. Sixty inches is the length
            our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> recommends for running, and four machines here have it: the Horizon 7.0 AT, the
            NordicTrack T Series 10, the Sole F63 and the ProForm Carbon TLX. The Horizon T101
            Connect and the <a href="/proform-carbon-tls-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>ProForm Carbon TLS</a> have 55-inch decks, which is fine for walking and
            occasional jogging and short for regular running. If storage decides it, our <a href="/black-friday-folding-treadmill-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmill Black Friday guide</a> compares folded footprints and weights.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Top speed: 12 mph rather than 10.</strong> Twelve mph is a five-minute mile, and
            ten mph a six-minute mile. Few home users run at either, but the ceiling still matters,
            because a machine designed for 12 mph is usually built for running throughout, and
            interval sessions at 8 to 10 mph sit comfortably inside its range rather than at the top
            of it. The same four machines reach 12 mph; the two walking machines stop at 10.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Width and capacity are the next checks.</strong> Five of the six have 20-inch
            belts; the Carbon TLS is 18 inches, which feels narrow at speed. Weight limits run from
            300 lb (T101 Connect, Carbon TLS, Carbon TLX) to 325 lb (7.0 AT, T Series 10, F63). A
            weight limit is a ceiling, not a comfortable operating point, so a heavier runner should
            prefer the 325 lb machines and leave margin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treat motor horsepower with care.</strong> It is the figure buyers most often
            compare and the one least comparable across brands. Horizon no longer publishes one for
            the 7.0 AT or T101 Connect; NordicTrack and a retailer describe the T Series 10 motor
            differently; ProForm footnotes its figures as laboratory measurements; Sole gives 3.0 HP
            for the F63. A continuous figure, a peak figure and a laboratory test figure are not the
            same thing, so they cannot be compared directly. For choosing between these machines, deck length, top speed, capacity and warranty are
            the more dependable signals.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you will run three or more times a week, our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a> guide covers what you gain above this price. If you will mostly walk, the two walking
            machines here are the better use of the money, and so is the evergreen <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills under $1,000</a> roundup, which covers the wider tier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="model-year" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Model-Year Clearance: Old Numbers, New Numbers</h2>
<p>
            The method page notes that the mid-range is where outgoing model years are most often
            cleared, and Black Friday is one of the points in the year when that happens. Clearance
            stock can be the best value in the category. It can also be the reason a price looks
            far better than the band allows. Every machine on this page has an older version or a
            near-namesake on sale somewhere, and these are the ones we found.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Horizon 7.0 AT.</strong> The $899 Black Friday deal in 2024 was for a listing
            titled "2024 Model". Horizon has sold the 7.0 AT under the same name across several
            years, and the current US product page identifies it as SKU HTM1457-01. If a listing
            names a model year or a different SKU, compare the console, warranty and specification
            with Horizon’s page before treating it as the same machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Horizon T101.</strong> Amazon search results for the T101 have shown the current
            T101 Connect alongside an older T101 marked as the old model, a T101-3 and a T101-04.
            The T101-05 was recalled in October 2022 over unexpected speed changes, fixed with a
            software update. Buy the T101 Connect, and check the serial number on any older or used
            unit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack T Series 10.</strong> A deal post from February 2025 showed a
            "NordicTrack T10" on Amazon at $599, described with a 10-inch screen, 12 mph, 12% incline
            and a 300 lb capacity. NordicTrack lists the current T Series 10 (NTL15425) at 325 lb,
            so that listing may have been an earlier version; we could not confirm which. The lesson
            is the same either way: a price far below every other recent price is a reason to check
            the model number, not a reason to rush.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>ProForm Carbon T7.</strong> This one is the clearest case. The Carbon T7 still
            appears on Amazon and in older buying guides, including an older version of our own
            under-$1,000 page, but it was not among the four treadmills on proform.com when our
            ProForm roundup checked on 13 October 2026. The current entry models are the Carbon TLS
            and Carbon TLX. A Carbon T7 offered at Black Friday is outgoing or older stock: check the
            seller, the warranty terms that apply to it, and whether you would rather have the
            current model at a similar price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole F63.</strong> Sole’s store described the F63 as "New for 2026" when we
            checked on 28 October, and our Sole roundup found the 2026 version has a white LED
            console with dial controls where older reviews describe an LCD, and a lower listed
            weight. An older F63 is not a worse machine for that, but the reviews you read may be
            describing the previous one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The working rule is simple. Find the model or SKU number on the brand’s own page, find it
            on the listing, and if they differ, compare deck, speed, incline, capacity and warranty
            line by line. An outgoing model at a genuine clearance price is often an excellent buy,
            provided you know that is what you are buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When the Deals Start in 2026</h2>
<p>
            Black Friday is Friday 27 November 2026 and <a href="/cyber-monday-treadmill-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Cyber Monday</a> is Monday 30 November. When we
            wrote this on 28 October, Amazon had not announced its 2026 Black Friday dates, and no
            brand on this page had published Black Friday prices. What follows is the usual shape of
            the season, not a schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Now to mid-November: record the baseline.</strong> The prices in the table above
            are a starting point. Check each machine you are considering once a week at Amazon and
            at the brand’s own store, with the delivery option you would choose, and write the
            numbers down with the date. Some promotions are already running — Sole’s store had $100
            off the F63 and <a href="/sole-f65-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>F65</a> on 28 October, and Horizon’s store offered the 7.0 AT for $934 with
            a code in mid-October — so a "Black Friday" price that matches one of these is not new.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The week before Thanksgiving.</strong> Amazon’s Black Friday event typically
            opens in the week before Thanksgiving rather than on the day, and brand stores often
            start their own promotions around the same time or earlier. Treadmill reductions
            frequently begin here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Thanksgiving to Cyber Monday (26–30 November).</strong> Prices on mainstream
            machines can move more than once across these days, and stock on the most heavily
            discounted model can run out. If a shortlisted machine reaches your ceiling, buying then
            is usually better than waiting for a lower number that may not come.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Into December.</strong> Freight delivery is slowest in late November and
            December. A 250 lb treadmill needed by a particular date should be ordered with the
            delivery estimate on the order page in mind, not the one you hope for. If nothing hits
            your price, the second half of January is usually the next good window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Mid-Range Treadmill on Amazon at Black Friday</h2>
<p>
            Every machine here can be bought through Amazon, and for four of them the button below
            each card goes straight to the listing we matched. The listing still needs reading,
            and a 250 to 330 lb freight box raises the stakes on every line of it. Our guide to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a> covers the full process; at Black Friday four things matter most.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the seller.</strong> The Horizon listings our Amazon roundup checked were
            sold and shipped by Amazon.com, which is the simplest case: Amazon’s return terms and the
            manufacturer’s warranty both apply. A third-party seller on the same product page may be
            offering older stock or a unit the brand will not cover. Read the "sold by" line before
            the price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the price in the cart.</strong> Several mid-range treadmill listings hide
            the price until the item is in your cart, usually because it is below the brand’s
            minimum advertised price. That is not a warning sign, but the figure in search results
            and the figure at checkout can differ, in either direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price the delivery you need.</strong> Free delivery on freight often means
            kerbside or the first dry area inside the door. Horizon’s own store offers delivery to
            the entrance or garage, room-of-choice delivery and white-glove assembly; on Amazon it depends on the listing. Compare
            delivered prices, not headline ones, across Amazon and the brand store.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Read the return terms for this item.</strong> Holiday return extensions do not
            always cover freight-delivered fitness equipment, and some brands charge for return
            shipping: ProForm’s own 30-day trial, for example, charged $249.99 for return shipping
            on the Carbon TLS when our review checked. Keep the box until you are sure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="also-watching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Also Worth Watching, and What We Left Out</h2>
<p>
            Several machines that belong in this price band are not cards on this page, usually
            because we could not confirm an Amazon US listing or because their normal price makes a
            sub-$1,000 Black Friday price unlikely. They are still worth knowing about.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole F65.</strong> Sole’s store had it at $1,299.99 against $1,399.99 on 28
            October. Reaching $1,000 would take a cut of around 23% from that price, which is
            possible but not something to plan around. Our Sole roundup also found that Sole’s 2026
            listing and earlier reviews disagree on its deck width, user capacity and console, and
            our older F65 review page does not match Sole’s current figures. Confirm those with Sole
            before paying the premium over the F63.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Horizon T202.</strong> The cheapest Horizon with a 60-inch deck and 12 mph, at
            $899 or $799 with a code on Horizon’s store on 13 October, with a 12% incline and one year
            of parts and labour cover. We could not confirm a current Amazon US listing for it, so it
            is not a card here; if you are happy to buy direct, it belongs on the same shortlist as
            the 7.0 AT.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bowflex T6.</strong> A 60-inch, 12 mph, 15% incline machine with lifetime frame
            and motor cover, listed at $1,299 regular and $999 on sale on bowflex.com when our review
            checked in early October. Our Amazon roundup could not find current Bowflex treadmills on
            Amazon, so it is a brand-store watch rather than an Amazon one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack T Series 8.</strong> Sold through retailers rather than
            nordictrack.com; Abt listed it at $999 on 14 October. NordicTrack’s own sheet and
            retailer listings disagree on some of its figures, so read our T Series review before
            choosing it over the T Series 10.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two notes on older pages. The site’s <a href="/best-treadmills-under-800/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills under $800</a> page and the older under-$1,000 roundup include motor figures and products that predate
            the checks used here, such as a 2.5 CHP figure for the T101 that Horizon no longer
            publishes. Where those pages and this one differ, the figures on this page are the more
            recent check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With Treadmill Deals Under $1,000</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from treating every sub-$1,000 treadmill as interchangeable, when the deal list mixes running machines, walking machines and older stock at similar prices.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a walking machine for running</h3>
              <p>A 55-inch deck and 10 mph ceiling can appear at the same price as a 60-inch, 12 mph machine in the same deal list. If you will run regularly, filter on deck length and top speed first and price second; a discounted walking machine is not a saving if you outgrow it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring the saving from the reference price</h3>
              <p>Mid-range treadmills often carry reference prices hundreds of dollars above what they sell for. Compare against the price you recorded in October and November, and against dated deal prices such as the $899 Black Friday 2024 price on the Horizon 7.0 AT, not against the strikethrough.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not checking the model number</h3>
              <p>Outgoing versions and near-namesakes are common in this band: older T101s, earlier T Series listings, and the ProForm Carbon T7, which is no longer in ProForm’s current range. Match the model or SKU number on the listing to the brand’s current page before you buy.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the delivered and ongoing cost</h3>
              <p>A 250 to 330 lb freight box may arrive kerbside, and a touchscreen machine may need a paid membership to be useful. Add the delivery tier you need and any subscription you will actually pay for before comparing a $950 deal with a $999 one.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            For a running treadmill under $1,000 this Black Friday, <strong class="text-white">the Horizon
            7.0 AT at $899 or less</strong> is the clearest buy, with the NordicTrack T Series 10 under
            $1,000 for anyone who wants a touchscreen and the Sole F63 at $999 or less for anyone who
            wants neither a screen nor a subscription. If you will mostly walk, the Horizon T101
            Connect at around $600 leaves money in the budget.
          </p>
          <p>
            Record the prices now, check the deck length, top speed and model number, and buy when a
            shortlisted machine meets your ceiling on a delivered basis. Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Black Friday method page</a> covers how to judge any deal, and the <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">under-$1,000 roundup</a> covers the wider tier.
          </p>
        </div>

